                Title="Routing and Navigation"
                Duration="8 min"
                Level="Intermediate" />
            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Basic Routing</h2>
                    <p>The code below shows how to declare routes for components using the <code>@@page</code> directive, how to capture route parameters and constraints, and how to navigate programmatically using <code>NavigationManager</code>.</p>
                    <p>These two routes demonstrate multiple route templates for a single component; the router matches the first suitable template. Be careful with overlapping templates (put more specific routes first) to avoid unexpected matches.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-razor">@@page "/products"
@@page "/products/list"  @@* Multiple routes *@@

&lt;h1&gt;Products&lt;/h1&gt;</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Route Parameters</h2>
                    <p>The snippet shows how to declare a route parameter (and optional parameters) and bind it to a property on the component using <code>[Parameter]</code>.</p>
                    <p>The <code>{Id:int}</code> segment enforces that the route parameter parses as an integer and will not match strings. If parsing fails the route won't match and the request will fall through to other routes (or the NotFound page), so validate inputs or provide fallback behavior.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-razor">@@page "/products/{Id:int}"

&lt;h1&gt;Product @@Id&lt;/h1&gt;

@@code {
    [Parameter]
    public int Id { get; set; }
}

@@* Optional parameter *@@
@@page "/products/{Category?}"

@@code {
    [Parameter]
    public string? Category { get; set; }
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Route Constraints</h2>
                    <p>Route constraints tell the router how to parse and validate parameters. Use them to avoid invalid routes and to get strongly-typed parameters in your components.</p>
                    <p>If a constraint does not match (e.g., a non-guid string for <code>:guid</code>), the router treats it as a non-match and continues searching other routes.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-razor">@@page "/users/{Id:int}"           @@* Integer *@@
@@page "/users/{Id:guid}"          @@* GUID *@@
@@page "/orders/{Date:datetime}"   @@* DateTime *@@
@@page "/items/{Active:bool}"      @@* Boolean *@@
@@page "/search/{Query:minlength(3)}" @@* Min length *@@</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Query String Parameters</h2>
                    <p>This example demonstrates how to read values from the query string into component parameters using <code>[SupplyParameterFromQuery]</code>.</p>
                    <p>Query values are URL-encoded; use nullable or default-valued properties to handle missing values, and remember that complex objects require manual parsing or a custom approach.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-razor">@@page "/search"

@@* URL: /search?query=blazor&amp;page=2 *@@

@@code {
    [SupplyParameterFromQuery]
    public string? Query { get; set; }

    [SupplyParameterFromQuery]
    public int Page { get; set; } = 1;
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>NavLink Component</h2>
                    <p>Use <code>NavLink</code> to render navigation links that apply an "active" class when the current URL matches the link.</p>
                    <p>Set <code>Match</code> to <code>All</code> to require an exact match, or <code>Prefix</code> to match the route and its subpaths (useful for parent nav items). CSS typically styles the <code>active</code> class to indicate the current page.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-razor">@@* Adds 'active' class when route matches *@@
&lt;NavLink href="/products" Match="NavLinkMatch.All"&gt;
    Products
&lt;/NavLink&gt;

&lt;NavLink href="/products" Match="NavLinkMatch.Prefix"&gt;
    Products (matches /products/*)
&lt;/NavLink&gt;</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Programmatic Navigation</h2>
                    <p>This shows how to inject <code>NavigationManager</code> and call <code>NavigateTo</code> for internal or external navigation (optionally forcing a full page load).</p>
                    <p>Use <code>forceLoad: true</code> to navigate to an external URL or to force a full reload; for internal SPA navigation prefer the default behavior so Blazor stays in control. You can also inspect <code>NavigationManager.Uri</code> to read the current URL.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-razor">@@inject NavigationManager Navigation

&lt;button @@onclick="GoToProducts"&gt;View Products&lt;/button&gt;

@@code {
    private void GoToProducts()
    {
        Navigation.NavigateTo("/products");
    }

    private void GoToProduct(int id)
    {
        Navigation.NavigateTo($"/products/{id}");
    }

    private void GoExternal()
    {
        Navigation.NavigateTo("https://google.com", forceLoad: true);
    }
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Not Found Page</h2>
                    <p>This snippet shows how to configure a NotFound view in <code>App.razor</code>, returning a friendly 404 message for unmatched routes.</p>
                    <p>Use <code>NotFound</code> to display custom messaging or redirect users back to a safe page; it's rendered when no route matches.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-razor">@@* In App.razor *@@
&lt;Router AppAssembly="@@typeof(App).Assembly"&gt;
    &lt;Found Context="routeData"&gt;
        &lt;RouteView RouteData="@@routeData" DefaultLayout="@@typeof(MainLayout)" /&gt;
    &lt;/Found&gt;
    &lt;NotFound&gt;
        &lt;LayoutView Layout="@@typeof(MainLayout)"&gt;
            &lt;h1&gt;404 - Page Not Found&lt;/h1&gt;
            &lt;p&gt;Sorry, the page you requested doesn't exist.&lt;/p&gt;
            &lt;a href="/"&gt;Go Home&lt;/a&gt;
        &lt;/LayoutView&gt;
    &lt;/NotFound&gt;
&lt;/Router&gt;</code></pre>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>@@page directive defines component routes.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Route parameters use {Name:type} syntax.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>NavigationManager enables programmatic navigation.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>NavLink adds active styling automatically.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>
            </div>
